<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">New Granada</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> was the Spanish name for the lands of present-day Colombia and Panama, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> of Granada founded in 1538. In 1717 it became the seat of the Viceroyalty of New Granada, separated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and including Venezuela and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>, re-established permanently in 1739. During the independence wars its patriots fought the royalists, and in 1819 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-angostura">Congress of Angostura</a> united it with Venezuela as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esztergom">Gran</a> Colombia.</p></div>
